Lemon law in California - known as the [Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act](https://library.nclc.org/book/consumer-warranty-law/126-california-song-beverly-consumer-warranty-act) - has many nuances. Nearly all consumers forced to navigate the complexities are doing so for the first time. Generally speaking, there are three common outcomes in California lemon law claims: a lemon law buyback from the manufacturer (refund), a replacement vehicle provided by the manufacturer, and a cash-and-keep settlement.

Today’s topic of selling a lemon car relates to the third scenario.

A [cash-and-keep settlement](/blog/cash-and-keep-settlement) (as the name implies) involves the manufacturer paying you a cash settlement and letting you keep the defective vehicle - assuming it meets the qualifications of California lemon law.

Some affected consumers take this option in hopes they can fix their defective vehicle and sell it to come out on top. Unfortunately, it’s not always that easy.

As an experienced lemon law California lawyer, I want to explain the process of selling a lemon car and what you need to know. Let’s get moving.

## Introduction: Understanding Your Options Per the State’s Lemon Laws

Choosing a cash-and-keep settlement for your lemon car provides you with certain freedoms, including the option to sell the vehicle.

When you and the manufacturer decide a vehicle is a lemon under California’s lemon law, it means the manufacturer’s certified repair facility cannot fix a defect after a reasonable number of attempts - and the lemon law case may move forward.

But this doesn’t always mean the defect is “unfixable”.

If you opt for the cash-and-keep settlement, the lemon vehicle is yours to do what you please - including repairing it and/or selling it.

However, once the vehicle is ruled a lemon under lemon law in California, it will be reflected in the vehicle’s title and history. Whether you’re considering selling to a dealer or pursuing a private sale, it’s best to operate with full transparency about your California lemon law claim to stay within the legal boundaries.

## Disclosure is Key

When selling a lemon car in California, transparency about the vehicle’s history and condition is not just ethical - it’s a legal requirement. If you plan to sell the defective vehicle, you must inform any potential buyer or dealer that the car has been deemed a lemon.

This disclosure ensures buyers are fully aware of the vehicle’s past issues, allowing them to make an informed decision. Failing to disclose this information could lead to legal repercussions down the line.

When you sell a lemon vehicle, California lemon law requires you to provide the following information on a letter-size paper:

- The year, make, and model of the vehicle - with the vehicle identification number (VIN)

- Indication the vehicle has been ruled a “lemon”.

- An explanation of the defect(s) on record.

This information should be provided regardless of who you sell the vehicle to.

## Opting for an “As Is” Sale

If you choose not to repair the vehicle’s defect before selling it, you can do so with an [“as is” agreement](https://www.autotrader.com/car-shopping/buying-a-used-car-what-does-as-is-mean-228533).

Selling your lemon car “as is” means that you’re selling the vehicle in its current condition, with all its known faults, and without any guarantee of its condition.

This option requires clear communication to the buyer that they’re accepting all existing problems with the car. An “as is” sale doesn’t negate the need for disclosure about the car’s lemon status, but it does clarify that you won’t be responsible for future repairs or issues.

## Selling to a Dealer

It’s not uncommon for used car dealerships to buy lemon cars from consumers to [sell them later](/blog/california-buyback-lemon-law-what-happens-to-lemon-vehicles). They may opt to repair the defect or simply resell it - clearly indicating the lemon status to buyers.

If you choose to sell your defective vehicle to a dealer, be prepared that a dealer’s offer might reflect the car’s troubled history under the lemon law in California, resulting in a lower price than you might hope for. Even in a dealer sale, full disclosure of the lemon status is necessary to ensure a transparent and ethical transaction.

Like any smart car buyer, they will look at the vehicle’s history and quickly find its lemon status. Just be upfront here - and be prepared to negotiate!

## Navigating a Private Sale

Selling a lemon car privately can be more challenging, requiring diligence and clear communication with potential buyers.

Some people choose to sell a lemon vehicle in a private sale to avoid negotiating with car dealerships - in hopes of getting a better price. Additionally, they might assume they don’t have to disclose the lemon status to a private buyer. This is an unethical move that can potentially land you in legal trouble for withholding information.

If you’re selling a lemon in a private sale, we highly recommend providing all relevant documentation, including the car’s service history and details of its lemon status. Even though you may fetch a higher price than a dealer may offer, it comes with a responsibility to make sure the buyer fully understands the vehicle’s condition.

## The Role of a Lemon Law California Lawyer

Consulting with a lemon law California lawyer is always a smart move when selling a lemon car. California lemon law attorneys know that you’re probably in this situation for the first time. Most will be happy to instruct you on the legal procedures required for selling a lemon car.

A CA lemon law attorney will provide advice on how to properly disclose the car’s lemon status, review any “as is” agreements to ensure they meet legal standards, and provide guidance on how to navigate the sale to protect yourself legally.

Documentation and Records

When preparing to sell your lemon car, gather all relevant documentation, including:

- The original purchase contract

- Repair orders and service records

- Any correspondence with the manufacturer or dealer regarding the lemon law claim

- Documentation of your cash-and-keep settlement

If you choose to repair the defective vehicle with a third-party mechanic, be sure to provide the records of the repair process - including the details of the issue, the remedy, and the results.

## Wrap Up: Making an Informed Decision

Selling a lemon car in California is a viable option if you’ve opted for a cash-and-keep agreement, but it requires careful consideration and adherence to legal obligations.

Disclosure of the vehicle’s lemon status, whether selling to a dealer or privately, is paramount to ensure a fair and transparent transaction. Working with a lemon law California lawyer can provide the guidance and support needed to navigate this process smoothly.

